# fast-ssim2 NEON vs forced-scalar — first tier-isolated measurement
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
command: cargo bench --bench tier_isolation
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native

## Result

| size       | NEON     | scalar   | speedup |
|------------|----------|----------|---------|
| 320x240    |   2.74ms |   4.75ms |  1.73x  |
| 1920x1080  |  72.14ms | 129.76ms |  1.80x  |
| 3840x2160  | 286.22ms | 519.68ms |  1.82x  |

No regression. But note the comparison against the sibling perceptual metric:
butteraugli measures 3.4-3.7x NEON on the same host
(butteraugli/benchmarks/neon_tier_isolation_2026-07-28.meta), roughly double.
Both are multi-scale XYB + blur pipelines, so a 2x difference in SIMD payoff
suggests parts of the SSIMULACRA2 pipeline are still scalar. That is a lead,
not a conclusion — attributing it needs a profile.

## Follow-up: downscale_by_2 de-clamping (same day)

`downscale_by_2` was fully scalar — a 5-deep nested loop with a per-tap
`.min()` clamp — and runs 12x per comparison (6 pyramid levels x 2 images).
Hoisting the clamps out of the interior (they are no-ops except on the final
row/column of an odd dimension) leaves a flat contiguous 2x2 sum that LLVM
autovectorises.

Isolated:   2.81ms -> 1.27ms per 5-level 1920x1080 pyramid = 2.21x
End-to-end:  72.14ms -> 71.30ms at 1920x1080               = 1.2%
            286.22ms -> 283.30ms at 3840x2160              = 1.0%
              2.74ms ->   2.70ms at 320x240                = 1.5%

NOTE the gap between those two lines. The isolated micro-benchmark predicted
~4.3% of total runtime; the real end-to-end saving is ~1.2%, because the
micro-benchmark re-cloned a cold buffer each iteration while the real pipeline
hits this function with the data already warm. The 4.3% figure was a
projection and it was wrong — only the end-to-end number is reported as the
result. Bit-identical output (verified vs the previous implementation at
1920x1080, 1921x1081, 17x5, 2x2: zero differing pixels; full reference-
conformance suite green).
